flsplash -- the FreeLFS boot screen

The one glossy thing in FreeLFS, on purpose: the logo of docs/logo.svg (three
bars tilted 6 degrees) as beveled, glossy green slabs with an extruded side
and a gel highlight, a soft glow behind, a faded mirror image on a dark floor,
a light band that sweeps across, and "FreeLFS" (IBM Plex Sans, spaced, one
size) fading in under it. About 3.8 s, then it holds.

How: nothing is drawn at boot, only composited. genart.c renders the layers
once at build time (bevel normals, diffuse and specular light, a fake studio
environment, the highlight, 6 x 6 or 4 x 4 supersampling) in six sizes,
deflated into flsplash.dat, which is built into the binary (about 0.5 MB).
At boot the size that fits the screen height is picked (up to 40% of it), so
nothing is ever scaled: no blur. The word is part of the art and never drawn
again, so the last frame is exactly what stays on screen until the login
screen takes over. Only the logo region is composited per frame, in
premultiplied integer math, two channels per 32 bit word. The clock is
CLOCK_MONOTONIC from the first picture: a slow machine drops frames, never time.

It draws into KMS dumb buffers (a legacy SETCRTC on every connected
connector), or /dev/fb0 when there is no DRM device. No compositor, no
libdrm: the ioctls are called directly. With two buffers each frame is a page
flip on the vertical blank (the next frame starts when the flip event
arrives); where flips do not work (simpledrm) the region alone is copied
into the buffer on screen, paced at 60 Hz.

  flsplash             started by rc.sysinit as root; forks, exits 0 always
  flsplash --handover  waits for the animation to end, drops DRM master (the
                       last picture stays in the scanout) and returns; flgreet
                       and the first-boot step of rc.sysinit run it before
                       flcomp. flsplash exits 6 s later.
  flsplash --text      the boot text now (also puts the console back to text)
  flsplash --render MS W H FILE.ppm
                       the picture at MS ms on a W x H screen, written to a
                       PPM; needs no screen (how to look at it while tuning
                       genart.c)

Signals: USR1 hands the screen over, USR2 or TERM show the text. Esc shows
the text too (tty0 is read in raw mode); a failed `step` sends USR2. Kernel
option `nosplash` turns it off. With KD_GRAPHICS set on tty0, the status lines
of rc go to the console buffer only (the serial console still gets them).

When the DRM device goes away (simpledrm giving way to the real driver) the
card is opened again and drawing continues at the right point: /dev and
/dev/dri are watched with inotify, vanished cards answer ENODEV, and mappings
of an unplugged card raise SIGBUS, which is caught. If no screen shows up
within 4 s it exits and the boot stays text. Nobody taking over within 3
minutes also means text.

Files in /run: flsplash.pid, .done (animation over), .released (handed over),
.log (what happened, in ms).

Build: make (genart needs FreeType and the IBM Plex Sans font, flsplash needs
zlib); make install (PREFIX, DESTDIR). The art file is generated, not kept in
the tree.

Wallpaper colours: `genart -W -a RRGGBB -w FILE` renders only the wallpaper (the 3D F without the
word) with the hue of the green material replaced (HSV: saturation and brightness stay, white
highlights stay white). The Makefile makes wallpaper-teal/blue/amber/orange/rose.flw this way next to
the green wallpaper.flw (same ids and colours as libflui's accents); Settings > Display > Wallpaper
offers them and flcomp loads the chosen one. The boot logo itself stays green.
